Claim: if there's a unique subarray of length `l`, there's one of length `l+1` (provided l < n). If unique subarray `nums[i..i+l-1]` exists and `i+l < n`, then `nums[i..i+l]` is unique. If `i+l == n` (it ends at the end), consider extending to the left: `nums[i-1..i+l-1]` — is that unique? If `nums[j..j+l] == nums[i-1..n-1]`, then `nums[j+1..j+l] == nums[i..n-1]`, contradicting uniqueness of `nums[i..n-1]` unless `j+1 == i`... but `j+1 == i` means `j == i-1`, same subarray. So yes, extending left works too when `i > 0`. If `i == 0` and `i+l == n`, then `l == n` which means the whole array is unique at length n, and l < n isn't possible. So the predicate "exists unique subarray of length l" is monotonic in l. Binary search works, but linear scan from l=1 with early termination could be worst-case O(n²) hashes total if answer is large (n/2 lengths × O(n) each). Binary search gives O(n log n).

To be safe against hash collisions, use double hashing or Python's built-in hash of tuples — but computing tuple per subarray is O(n·l). Rolling hash with two mod primes is safer. Or use one 64-bit mod (2^64 via natural overflow emulation) — collision unlikely but let's use double mod to be safe.

